# KNOWN GAP — the STRING-payload cell of the `auto`-peel, which fails one stage
# EARLIER than its siblings and for a second reason.
#
# Same root as `auto_result_peel_missing_method_build_failure.gg`: in a
# propagating context `auto j = s.fetch(g)` lowers to a PEEL, so LOWERING binds
# `j` to the payload while the CHECKER binds it to the `Result` (D45 pin 6 — an
# unmarked position never peels, so the checker is right; `todo/t0434`'s
# `expr_types` plumbing is the closure).
#
# WHAT MAKES THIS CELL ITS OWN FIXTURE: the payload is a `String`, and the
# String dispatch ladder does not miss cleanly — it CONCATENATES a runtime
# symbol name from the method name and emits `gorget_str_is_error(...)`. So the
# build dies in the C COMPILER with
#
#     error: implicit declaration of function 'gorget_str_is_error';
#            did you mean 'gorget_str_is_empty'?
#
# rather than at the linker. That invented-runtime-symbol behaviour is the
# `todo/t0987` family, and it is why this cell is separated: it would MASK its
# siblings' link diagnostics if they shared a fixture (the C compile never
# reaches the link stage), and the two failure modes will move independently.
#
# Measured 2026-09-03: before the `t1017` dispatch fix this built and printed
# `str ok | boom | done` — wrong, because `j.is_error()` folded to
# `Constant::Bool(false)` and the user's error block was silently discarded
# (`todo/t0947`'s Core #10 filing). After, it does not build.
#
# INTENDED (D45 pin 6 — `j` binds the `Result`, `j.is_error()` is the Result's
# own tag check):
#   str ok
#   handled str
#   done
struct Sock:
    int h

equip Sock:
    Result[String, String] fetch_str(self, String g):
        if g == "bad":
            return Error("boom")
        return Ok("payload")

Result[bool, String] use_str(String g):
    Sock s = Sock(1)
    auto j = s.fetch_str(g)
    if j.is_error():
        return Error("handled str")
    return Ok(true)

void main():
    Result[bool, String] a = use_str("good")
    if a.is_error():
        print(a.unwrap_error())
    else:
        print("str ok")
    Result[bool, String] b = use_str("bad")
    if b.is_error():
        print(b.unwrap_error())
    print("done")
